{"id":18,"title":"Conscience Point","slug":"conscience-point","publication_status":"published","summary":"Conscience Point is remembered locally as the 1640 landing place of English settlers in Southampton, but the harbor and marsh landscape was already part of Shinnecock homeland. Its history should begin with Shinnecock presence, land use, diplomacy, and survival before turning to the English settlement story preserved by Southampton institutions.","address_label":"40.94082600663445, -72.41531562011022","site_type":null,"geometry_surface":null,"geometry_cleanup_status":null,"map_geometry_source":"imported_wp_go_maps","map_fill_color":null,"map_opacity":null,"map_icon":"3b60bd8c-0a3b-40ee-b485-18465fb30355","listing_image_file":null,"listing_image_url":"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/07/5I5A4924-Edit-1.jpg","listing_image_thumb_url":"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/07/5I5A4924-Edit-1-768x162.jpg","listing_image_alt":"5I5A4924-Edit","show_print_purchase":true,"activity_pin_until":null,"activity_pin_label":null,"activity_pin_title":null,"activity_pin_preview":null,"activity_update_date":"2026-08-25T04:43:11","activity_update_summary":"Consolidated the existing dated chronology into cited Historic Moments and removed the duplicate unformatted history block.","last_reviewed":"2026-09-20T00:00:00","known_plant_species":null,"ancestral_territory":137,"ancestral_territory_note":"Shown in relation to Shinnecock Ancestral Land on the current ancestral territory map.","why_this_matters":"Long before English colonists waded ashore at Conscience Point in 1640, the Shinnecock people had thrived for centuries around this highly productive, marsh-bordered harbor. Re-centering the deep Indigenous occupation of famous colonial landing sites challenges the persistent myth that Long Island's history only began with European arrival.","introduction_title":null,"introduction_content":null,"oral_history_title":null,"oral_history_content":null,"history_title":null,"history_content":"<section data-source-section=\"shinnecock-elders-2014\"><h3>Resources remembered by Shinnecock elders</h3><p>The Shinnecock Environmental Department’s 2014 catalogue names Conscience Point in its resource accounts. These sections preserve the places named in that 2014 community record. Present-day access, abundance, and harvesting rules may differ.</p><h4><a href=\"#wiki/coastal-fish\">Alewives</a></h4><p>The catalogue describes returning alewives as a sign of spring and remembers their use in planting. Seasonal fish movements connected the water with work in gardens. <a href=\"https://nativelongisland.com/assets/documents/shinnecock-elders-natural-resources-2014.pdf#page=2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PDF p. 2</a>.</p><h4><a href=\"#wiki/coastal-fish\">Atlantic salmon in elders’ memories</a></h4><p>Elders remembered catching fish they called salmon on the ocean side when they were young. They also recalled older fishers going to Conscience Point when shad moved upstream. <a href=\"https://nativelongisland.com/assets/documents/shinnecock-elders-natural-resources-2014.pdf#page=2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PDF p. 2</a>.</p><p><a href=\"#wiki/shinnecock-elders-natural-resources-2014\">Explore all resources and places in the catalogue</a>.</p><p><a href=\"https://nativelongisland.com/assets/documents/shinnecock-elders-natural-resources-2014.pdf#page=2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Shinnecock Environmental Department, Natural Resources Identified by Shinnecock Elders, April 4, 2014, PDF p. 2. Page numbers count the cover as PDF page 1.</a></p></section>\n<h3>Photographs and archival images</h3><figure><a href=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/07/conscience-point-photo.jpg\"><img src=\"https://nativelongisland.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/07/conscience-point-photo.jpg\" alt=\"Conscience Point and Monument (Home Hills in Distant) Date taken and photographer 